.TH SELECT 3crunch "" "Crunch" "Crunch Library Manual"
.SH NAME
select \- wait for file descriptor readiness
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <sys/select.h>
.PP
.BI "int select(int " count ", fd_set* " readfds ", fd_set* " writefds ,
.BI "           fd_set* " exceptfds ", struct timeval* " timeout ");"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Waits for readiness on descriptors numbered from zero through
.I count
minus one. Each set may be zero or point to a writable
.BR fd_set .
Use FD_ZERO, FD_SET, FD_CLR and FD_ISSET to initialize, change and inspect
sets; descriptors must be nonnegative and below FD_SETSIZE (1024).
Nonready bits are cleared. Linux uses the supplied count and bitmap sizes;
the caller must provide sufficient storage.
.PP
The optional writable
.B struct timeval
has
.B time_t tv_sec
and
.B suseconds_t tv_usec
members. A zero pointer waits indefinitely; a zero timeval polls without
waiting. Linux may modify the timeout, including on interruption, so reset it
before reuse.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns the total number of ready bits across all sets, zero on timeout, or
\-1 with
.B errno
set on failure. Success leaves errno unchanged.
.SH ERRORS
Include
.BR <errno.h> .
Errors include
.BR EBADF ,
.BR EFAULT ,
.BR EINTR ,
.BR EINVAL ,
and
.BR ENOMEM .
.SH NOTES
Crunch errno is process\-wide. Existing raw bitmap callers must convert
their pointer to fd_set* and provide sufficient storage.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR poll (3crunch),
.BR select (2)
